15719176,2025-09-04,Debt collection,Payday loan debt,Attempts to collect debt not owed,Debt is not yours,"I received a collection notice from American Coradius International LLC for an alleged business loan originally issued by XXXX. After reviewing the account, I formally disputed the debt and requested validation under the Fair Debt Collection Practices Act ( FDCPA ), 15 U.S.C. 1692g. 

Through my research, I found that American Coradius is a non-bank debt collector, and therefore not exempt from New York States usury laws. The loan carried an APR of 28.34 %, which exceeds New Yorks civil usury cap ( 16 % ) and criminal usury cap ( 25 % ). 

This position is supported by the Second Circuit Court of Appeals in Madden v. Midland Funding, LLC, 786 F.3d 246 ( 2d Cir. 2015 ), which held that non-bank entities that purchase debt from national banks are not entitled to the federal preemption that protects banks from state usury laws. That means this collector can not lawfully enforce a loan in violation of New York usury limits.

In addition, there was an active UCC-1 lien on file at the time the loan was originated. Extending credit under that condition appears predatory and unconscionable.

I requested : Full debt validation ; Proof of chain of title and assignment ; Legal authority to collect in New York ; Suspension of collection activity and credit reporting while the debt is disputed.

To date, they have failed to comply. I am filing this complaint because I believe this debt is legally unenforceable under state and federal law, and the collection attempt violates both FDCPA and New York consumer protection standards.",,American Coradius International LLC,NY,12047,Servicemember,Web,2025-09-04,Closed with explanation,Yes
